{"entries":{"chapter-80-study-respiratory-system-and-selected-pulmonary-disorders|Chapter 80 Study: Respiratory System and Selected Pulmonary Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Lung function reflects lung tissue, airways, respiratory muscles, chest wall and cardiovascular function; normal decline need not reach symptomatic disease (p1229).":["u3"],"copy|Senescence limits tissue repair; a persistent senescence-associated secretory phenotype promotes inflammation and fibrosis (p1230).":["u4"],"copy|Fewer naive T cells, impaired ciliary clearance and altered extracellular matrix increase vulnerability to infection and stress (p1230).":["u5"],"copy|Spirometry measures exhaled flow and volume; a lower FEV1/FVC ratio indicates greater obstruction. Spirometry alone cannot measure total lung volume (p1230).":["u6"],"copy|With aging, FEV1 falls more than FVC; the ratio declines. Opposing lung and chest-wall changes leave total lung capacity relatively constant (p1231).":["u7"],"copy|Diffusing capacity declines with capillary loss and ventilation–perfusion mismatch (p1231).":["u8"],"copy|IPF is more common after 60; consider it with insidious progressive exertional dyspnea, cough and basal fine crackles (p1231).":["u9"],"copy|IPF usually reduces total lung capacity and diffusing capacity. Chest CT may show the usual interstitial pneumonia pattern: reticulation, honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis (p1231).":["u10"],"copy|Refer suspected or confirmed IPF to pulmonology. Nintedanib and pirfenidone slow progression of restriction but do not reverse existing scarring; nausea and diarrhea may limit treatment (p1231).":["u11"],"copy|Antifibrotic initiation and continuation require individual decisions; immediate symptom or quality-of-life improvement may not occur. The chapter does not specify their doses (p1231).":["u12"],"copy|Avoid tobacco and pollution; address vaccination, healthy weight, cardiovascular disease and osteoporosis. Consider oxygen supplementation if hypoxic (p1229, p1232–1233).":["u13"],"copy|Consider pulmonary/cardiac rehabilitation after acute illness in patients with chronic cardiopulmonary disease; recovery may otherwise be slow and incomplete (p1233).":["u14"],"copy|Aging does not simply reduce every lung volume: total lung capacity remains relatively constant while airflow and diffusion decline (p1231).":["u15"],"copy|IPF is primarily fibrotic: the chapter centers treatment on antifibrotics rather than chronic immunosuppression; consider transplant evaluation for suitable patients (p1231).":["u16"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u17"],"copy|2023-Jun Q67":["u18"],"copy|2024-May Q28":["u19"],"copy|2024-Sep Q39":["u20"],"copy|2026-Jun Q42":["u21"],"copy|CHAPTER 80s / HAZZARD":["u22"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 80 - Respiratory System and Selected Pulmonary Disorders; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u23"],"copy|Aging & physiology":["u24"],"copy|Assessment":["u25"],"copy|Management & drugs":["u26"],"copy|Exam traps":["u27"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (4):":["u28"],"copy|Eight past-exam questions are linked to this chapter.":["u28"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Compare the patient's ratio with its age-appropriate lower limit and interpret it alongside symptoms and exposures before diagnosing airway disease.":["u49"],"copy|Full inflation is a third condition: reduced inward recoil favors a higher TLC, while vertebral height loss and stiffer rib joints restrict expansion (Ch 80 pp1230–1231; Ch 81 p1237 on increased RV and FRC).":["u57"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The size and detection of the FRC change vary by population.":["u56"],"copy|Chronic steroids have no established survival benefit in stable IPF (p1231).":["u86"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Oxygen corrects hypoxemia, but the mortality evidence for long-term oxygen in severely hypoxemic COPD cannot simply be applied to IPF.":["u88"],"copy|The chapter lists gastrointestinal adverse effects, including nausea and diarrhea, with antifibrotic therapy (p1231).":["u135"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Diarrhea is especially common with nintedanib and nausea with pirfenidone.":["u90"],"copy|A promise that side effects will be minor offers less information to the individual patient.":["u94"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): United States drug update.":["u100"],"copy|CHAPTER 80 / HAZZARD":["u22"]},"maxId":135,"sequenceOffsets":[[136,-107],[156,-106],[162,-104],[190,-103],[191,-102],[192,-101],[195,-100],[200,-99],[234,0]]}
